nt/hare":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/hare","n":"Hare","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Hares are mammals belonging to the genus Lepus. They are herbivores and live solitarily or in pairs. They nest in depressions in the ground called forms, and their young are able to fend for themselves shortly after birth. The genus includes the largest lagomorphs. Most are fast runners with long, powerful hind legs, and large ears that dissipate body heat. Hare species are native to Africa, Eurasia and  North America. A hare less than one year old is called a leveret. A group of hares may be called either a husk or a down."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-hare"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hare"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Andrajos","dish/andrajos"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cod as food","ingredient/cod-as-food"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Rabbit","ingredient/rabbit"],["Rabbit meat","ingredient/rabbit-meat"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3f/Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg/500px-Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg","w":500,"h":701,"by":"Bernard DUPONT from FRANCE","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scrub_Hare_(Lepus_saxatilis)_close-up_(30544290256)_(2).jpg"}},"ingredient/engraulis":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/engraulis","n":"Engraulis","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Engraulis is a genus of anchovies, currently containing 9 species. Species in 